                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Aut( CovBund(X) \stackrel{F_x}{\to} \infty Grpd )
                  \\simeq
                  \\Omega_x \Pi(X) =: \Omega^{geom}_x X
                  \\,.
          %h3#InTermsOfMonodromyReferences References
          %p
            The idea that geometric homotopy groups of generalized
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaves
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stack
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-stack"} ∞-stack
            = succeed "-stacks" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck%27s+Galois+theory"} Grothendieck's Galois theory
            and the notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group+of+a+topos"} fundamental group of a topos
          %ul
            %li
              Marta Bunge,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Galois groupoids and covering morphisms in topos theory
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/viewdoc/summary?doi=10.1.1.15.7071"} links
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pursuing+Stacks"} Pursuing Stacks
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Grothendieck"} Grothendieck
            talked about how this 1-categorical situation generalizes to
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-stack"} ∞-stack
          %p
            After
            = succeed "," do
              %em Pursuing Stacks
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+group"} homotopy group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            may be recovered from the behaviour of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+constant+%E2%88%9E-stack"} locally constant ∞-stack
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            was
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bertrand+Toen"}> Bertrand Toen
              ,
              %em Toward a Galoisian interpretation of homotopy theory
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0007157"}> arXiv:0007157
              )
          %p This has a followup construction in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bertrand+Toen"} Bertrand Toen
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gabriele+Vezzosi"} Gabriele Vezzosi
              %em Segal topoi and stacks over Segal categories
              , Proceedings of the
              %p
                Program Stacks, Intersection theory and Non-abelian Hodge Theory, MSRI, Berkeley, January-May 2002 (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0212330"}> arxiv:math/0212330
                ).
          %p Very similar constructions and statement then appeared in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pietro+Polesello"} Pietro Polesello
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ingo+Waschkies"} Ingo Waschkies
                %em Higher monodromy
                , Homology, Homotopy and Applications, Vol. 7(2005), No. 1, pp. 109-150 (
                %a{:href => "http://www.intlpress.com/HHA/v7/n1/a7/v7n1a7.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"}> Mike Shulman
                ,
                %em Parametrized spaces model locally constant homotopy sheaves
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0706.2874"}> arXiv:0706.2874
                )
          %p and, building on that, in example 1.8 of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Denis-Charles+Cisinski"}> Denis-Charles Cisinski
              ,
              %em Locally constant functors
              , Math. Proc. Camb. Phil. Soc. , 147 (
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.univ-toulouse.fr/~dcisinsk/lcmodcat3.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            Notably the article by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pietro+Polesello"} Pietro Polesello
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ingo+Waschkies"} Ingo Waschkies
            makes fully explicit the observation that
            %em locally
            constant
            = succeed "-stacks" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %em constant
            = succeed "-stack" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            = succeed "-groupoid" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mi Grpd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n Grpd
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Π
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi LConst
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\Pi \dashv LConst)
            into the picture, as we do here.
          %p
            It was pointed out to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"} Urs Schreiber
            by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Williamson"} Richard Williamson
            that these constructions should generalize from topological spaces to objects in any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            = succeed "-toposes" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            comes canonically equipped with a notion of
            %span.newWikiWord
              homotopy ∞-groupoid
              %a{:href => "/schreiber/new/homotopy+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ?
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi(X)
            of every object
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∈
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \in \mathbf{H}
          %h2#Paths In terms of concrete paths
          %p …
          %h3#references_3 References
          %p
            The following references discuss fundamental groupoids of an entire
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            constructed from concrete
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+object"} interval object
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/petit+topos"} petit
            over-toposes over a given object in an ambient
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gros+topos"} gros topos
          %p
            The construction of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+groupoid"} fundamental groupoid
            of a topos from
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+object"} interval object
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ieke+Moerdijk"}> Ieke Moerdijk
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gavin+Wraith"} Gavin Wraith
              %em Connected locally connected toposes are path-connected
              , Transactions of the AMS, volume 295, number 2, (1986)
          %p The comparison of this construction with the one by monodromy/Galois theory is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marta+Bunge"}> Marta Bunge
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ieke+Moerdijk"} Ieke Moerdijk
              %em On the construction of the Grothendieck fundamental group of a topos by paths
              , J. Pure and Applied Algebra, 116 (1997)
          %h2#GeomExamples Examples
          %h3#Pi0Ofsheafontopspace
            Geometric
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_0
            of a sheaf on a locally connected topological space
          %p
            Here we discuss the 0-th geometric homotopy group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 0
                  %mo :
                  %mi Sh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_0 : Sh(X) \to Set
            of objects in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} sheaf topos
            in terms of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_0
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constant+sheaf"} constant sheaf
            functor. This is a special case of the more general situation discussed in
            %a{:href => "Pi0InLocConTop"} Pi0 of a general object in a locally connected topos
            below.
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            be a sufficiently nice
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
          .un_proposition
            %h6#observation Observation
            %p
              There is a triple of
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor"} adjoint functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi LConst
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi Sh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mover
                        %mover
                          %mo →
                          %mi Γ
                        %mover
                          %mo ←
                          %mi LConst
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi Π
                            %mn 0
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (\Pi_0 \dashv LConst \dashv \Gamma) \;\;\;
                    \:
                    \\;\;\; Sh(X) \stackrel{\overset{\Pi_0}{\to}}{\stackrel{\overset{LConst}{\leftarrow}}{\overset{\Gamma}{\to}}}
                    Set
            %p where
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi LConst
                        %mo ⊣
                        %mi Γ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (LConst \dashv \Gamma)
                  is the usual
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+section"} global section
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphism"} geometric morphism
                  with
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi LConst
                          %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LConst_S
                  the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constant+sheaf"} constant sheaf
                  of
                  = succeed "s" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+constant+function"} locally constant function
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi Set
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \in Set
                  and
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Π
                        %mo :
                        %mi Sh
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %mi Set
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi : Sh(X) \to Set
                  is
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
                  to
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi LConst
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LConst
                  and sends each sheaf
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                  to the set of connected components of the corresponding
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/etale+space"} etale space
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi p
                            %mi A
                          %mo :
                          %mi Et
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi A
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo →
                          %mi X
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_A : Et(A) \to X
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Π
                          %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
